---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ExecutionTrace from "../components/visual/ExecutionTrace.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

const festival = (await getCollection("work")).find(
  (entry) => entry.data.name === "Festival",
);

if (!festival) {
  throw new Error("Festival work entry is missing");
}
---

<Base
  title="Work — Obedience Corp"
  description="Forward deployed engagements, and Festival, the open-source system that runs them."
  current="/work"
>
  <section class="inner-hero inner-hero-split work-hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">SELECTED WORK</p>
      <h1>Work you can inspect, run, and judge for yourself.</h1>
      <p>
        Obedience Corp builds in the open where it can, and under NDA where it
        can’t. Festival is public and runnable today; the client work is
        described without naming the companies.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ExecutionTrace title="PUBLIC ARTIFACT / FESTIVAL" compact />
  </section>

  <section class="work-case">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">01 / OPEN SOURCE</p>
        <h2>{festival.data.name}</h2>
      </div>
      <p>{festival.data.license?.toUpperCase()} · AVAILABLE NOW</p>
    </header>

    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          Keep the way you work across agents and projects.
        </p>
        <p>
          A Festival camp workspace keeps plans, research, scripts, and workflow
          templates beside separately linked project repositories. You set the
          goal and review the plan. Your agent follows the steps with <code
            >fest next</code
          >, records results, and returns at the review points you set. A fresh
          session reads the saved state and instructions before continuing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project includes the <code>camp</code> and <code>fest</code>{" "}
          command-line tools and works alongside shell-capable agents such as Claude
          Code and Codex.
        </p>
        <p>
          The public CodeSignal Practice Simulator shows the result of three
          completed festivals: a Python engine, browser IDE, and practice
          library. Its repository includes the plans, review findings, and
          verification records. One review found that repeated edits could hide
          older saved versions despite passing browser tests. The record shows
          the failing regression tests, the correction, and verification that a
          user could restore an older version.
        </p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a href="https://fest.build/stories/codesignal-practice-simulator"
            >Inspect the completed build ↗</a
          >
          <a href={festival.data.url}>Product site ↗</a>
          <a href="https://docs.fest.build">Documentation ↗</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/Obedience-Corp/festival">Source ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>STATUS</dt>
          <dd>Public</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>LICENSE</dt>
          <dd>{festival.data.license}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>SURFACE</dt>
          <dd>Command line</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>FOCUS</dt>
          <dd>Context, planning, execution, verification</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-case" id="clipsync">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">02 / PUBLIC ENGINEERING</p>
        <h2>Copy on one computer. Paste on another.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>CLIPSYNC · EXPERIMENTAL</p>
    </header>
    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          ClipSync shares plain-text clipboards between trusted Mac and Linux
          computers.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal is familiar; the difficult work is in the details. Which
          devices may receive a copy? What happens when a service restarts? Can
          an update preserve the existing clipboard history?
        </p>
        <p>
          The public repair record follows those questions through authenticated
          connections, recovery checks, and tests that preserve existing history
          keys. It records isolated clipboard tests and an installed Mac–Arch
          Linux setup with automatic reconnection after service restarts.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also states what remains: broader desktop compatibility,
          sleep/wake, and login/logout acceptance. Lance built this open-source
          project for his own use. The public record lets you assess the
          implementation and its remaining limits.
        </p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a href="https://github.com/lancekrogers/clipsync"
            >See the app and its scope ↗</a
          >
          <a
            href="https://github.com/lancekrogers/clipsync/blob/main/docs/REPAIR_VALIDATION.md"
            >Read the verification and limits ↗</a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div><dt>USER GOAL</dt><dd>Move copied text between computers</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>WORK</dt><dd>Desktop integration, device trust, recovery</dd>
        </div>
        <div><dt>EVIDENCE</dt><dd>Public source and recorded checks</dd></div>
        <div><dt>STATUS</dt><dd>Experimental; release checks remain</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-case">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">03 / PRODUCT</p>
        <h2>Festival Activity</h2>
      </div>
      <p>MACOS AND LINUX · PAID</p>
    </header>

    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          A desktop timeline of the work in your camps, built on your machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          Festival Activity reads your git history and your Festival progress
          and shows you the shape of a month, a quarter, or a single afternoon,
          across every camp you have. It reads and nothing else: it does not
          edit your documents, run agents, or send anything anywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is the one thing here you buy rather than clone. Festival itself
          stays free and open.
        </p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a href="/festival-activity">Festival Activity →</a>
          <a href="/festival-activity/terms">Terms of sale</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>STATUS</dt>
          <dd>Paid, one purchase</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>RUNS ON</dt>
          <dd>macOS and Linux</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>NEEDS</dt>
          <dd>Camp and Fest, installed free</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>SENDS</dt>
          <dd>Nothing about your work</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-case client-work">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">04 / CLIENT WORK</p>
        <h2>Under NDA</h2>
      </div>
      <p>2025 · NAMES WITHHELD</p>
    </header>

    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          Three startups launched products built with these systems.
        </p>
        <p>
          The engagements are covered by agreements that do not allow us to name
          the companies or show the code, so we describe the shape of the work
          instead. In each case the same system carried it: a defined scope,
          context kept in files the client owns, execution across many sessions,
          and verification before anything shipped.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a reference matters before you commit, ask on a call. What we can
          share, we share directly. Festival above is the part you can audit
          line by line without asking anyone’s permission.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>ENGAGEMENTS</dt>
          <dd>3 startups, 2025</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>OUTCOME</dt>
          <dd>Products launched</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>DISCLOSURE</dt>
          <dd>Names and code under NDA</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>SAME SYSTEM</dt>
          <dd>Scope, context, execution, verification</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-proof">
    <p class="section-label">WHAT IT PROVES</p>
    <div>
      <h2>Not a concept site. A working system built under load.</h2>
      <p>
        Festival grew out of using AI agents every day across software,
        research, and company operations. The code, documentation, and ongoing
        field notes are public so the work can be evaluated on substance.
      </p>
      <a href="/blog/nine-months-of-festival"
        >Read the nine-month field report →</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-cta">
    <p class="section-label">APPLIED ENGAGEMENTS</p>
    <h2>Need this kind of reliability inside your own AI workflow?</h2>
    <a class="action action-primary" href="/#engagements"
      >See how an engagement works →</a
    >
  </section>
</Base>
